WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.270
Cooking with charcoal on small stoves

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.393
called jiko is commonplace
in sub-Saharan Africa.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.750
For those on low or unpredictable incomes

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:23.580
charcoal is readily available

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.313
and easier to source than
other fuels like gas.

00:00:43.627 --> 00:00:45.360
50 Kenyan shillings

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.223
is roughly half a US dollar.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.963
Cheap charcoal is a
vital part of many lives.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.920
Because that's the source
of energy people use,

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.050
there's an entire
industry that's been born

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:01.800
to cut down forests, make
charcoal, and sell it to people.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.680
As much as $47 billion worth of charcoal

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.250
is sold across Africa every year.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.810
And while many ordinary people

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.460
benefit from this economy,

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.983
it's also having a more negative impact.

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:25.080
Charcoal production is the
leading driver of deforestation

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.353
in Africa and is also
blamed for shortening lives.

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.200
This indoor air pollution actually kills

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.880
as many as 4 million people,

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.740
according to the United Nations.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.070
You also have to cut down
the trees from active forests

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.940
to be able to burn the
wood for energy uses

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.060
and that means that
releases carbon dioxide.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.890
And because you're deforesting,

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.800
there is carbon that's stored
in the soil in the forest,

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.300
which is also released.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.023
So it's bad for health,
it's bad for the planet.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.250
One Nairobi-based startup
says it can disrupt

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:01.680
this huge market by replacing the charcoal

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:03.840
with a cleaner burning bioethanol

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:06.093
that would also save African forests.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:10.950
We look at the potential for a solution

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.220
across 60 countries across Africa,

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.110
South and Central America, and Asia.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.750
That is where we see a
lot of use of firewood

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.763
and charcoal and a lot of deforestation.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.860
Koko Networks sources its fuel

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.223
from sugar processing plants
like this one in western Kenya.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:32.760
Rather than chopping down
trees to make firewood

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.710
or charcoal, the sugar molasses

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.743
that's converted into
ethanol is a waste byproduct.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.800
Koko distributes this cleaner burning fuel

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.260
to a network of so-called "Koko Points"

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:46.620
in densely populated areas in Kenya.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:48.990
The machines, like ATMs for cooking fuel,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:52.260
are placed in general convenience stores.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.570
There, customers insert a smart canister

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:57.658
and, using a PIN code, fill
up as much as they need.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:01.830
Once the canister's been
filled, it's plugged

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:05.523
into a special two hob cooker
designed with safety in mind.

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:08.670
One of the leading causes of fires,

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.500
especially in informal settlements,

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.293
comes from charcoal fires
and kerosene stoves.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.200
So from cutting down air pollution,

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:58.050
bioethanol is quite clean because it burns

00:03:58.050 --> 00:04:00.540
without producing all
this particulate matter

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.270
that is usually breathed
in by women mostly,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.740
who are doing the cooking,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.670
and then it goes all parts of
the body, even to the brain.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:12.630
So, there is no doubt that
cutting down on air pollution

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.753
by using these alternative
fuels is a good idea.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.260
For customers switching to a cleaner fuel

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:22.530
like Koko's bioethanol can
make a huge difference,

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.810
and not just to their health.

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:26.880
The system allows canisters to be filled

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.170
by as little as 30 Kenyan
shillings, about 25 US cents,

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.060
so if money's tight day-to-day,

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.733
a customer can buy just
enough for that one meal.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.700
The ethanol is cheaper than charcoal

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.710
and competes on price with kerosene,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.543
which the government subsidizes.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:48.600
The system also appears to
work well for shopkeepers

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.343
who host the Koko Point too.

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.730
Koko says one in four Nairobi households

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:16.383
are now cooking with their fuel.

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.670
According to data from the United Nations,

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:22.720
there's a reduction of over five tons

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.840
of greenhouse gas emissions per household

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.000
per year when changing from charcoal

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.803
to a bioethanol like Koko.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.550
When you're talking
about 585,000 households

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:38.790
and these are people who've
converted from using charcoal,

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.990
we've provided them with a
guaranteed fuel purchase.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:45.012
So you're guaranteed
that you will go to your

00:05:45.012 --> 00:05:46.950
Koko Point, you will get fuel.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.650
No matter how much money you
have, we're within reach.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.920
Of course, we have to be
careful about bioethanol

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.660
and how it's sourced.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:55.920
Right now it's being sourced

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.220
from the waste produced
in sugar cane processing.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.800
But if the demand for this product grows,

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:05.070
we need to be careful that we
don't end up using cropland

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.580
for the solution, because
there is not enough land

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:09.450
to be able to feed

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.650
all the billions of
people around the world.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.480
Koko wants to target countries

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.510
all over the world where
populations rely on charcoal

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.430
or wood-burning fuel.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:32.190
But to do so, they say
they need financial support

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.440
to keep their products competitive,

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.120
as well as long-term guarantees

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:39.060
to maintain and attract investment.

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:43.230
We want fiscal incentives in
the form of tax exemptions

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.080
on all our hardware and all our fuel.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:49.530
And more importantly,
we need carbon credits

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:51.930
to remain the property of Koko Networks.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:54.540
That is important because
we use the carbon credits

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:58.800
to subsidize both the
cooker and the bioethanol,

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:00.720
and that is why we tell the government,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.520
give us the leeway,
give us the exemptions,

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.310
so that we are able to
invest a lot faster,

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:07.980
get to a lot of these household
sooner rather than later,

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.840
because we are seeing the
impact of climate change,

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.180
we are seeing what we are
experiencing right now.

00:07:12.180 --> 00:07:13.800
So the sooner they give us the exemptions,

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.750
the sooner we are able
to make the investment,

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:18.600
the sooner Kenyans can start
experiencing the benefits.

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.290
Next year, they're hoping
to start operations

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:25.200
in Rwanda, where the government's
agreed to waive taxes

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:26.730
and import duties,

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:29.700
a model Koko says is essential
to getting cleaner fuel

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.160
into more households to
level the playing field

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.803
with charcoal, which is entirely untaxed.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:40.200
Any kind of startup coming
out with a new technology

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.140
has to compete with existing
solutions in the market.

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:46.650
And as a new technology, it's
not always going to be able

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:49.590
to be as cheap as what's
available on the market.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:52.590
So sometimes it helps if
governments can step in.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:55.080
In this case, many
governments have stepped in

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:58.800
to cut taxes on fuel,
and that goes a long way

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:02.010
in making the product
competitive in the market.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:04.410
But there's another
challenge for startups,

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:07.140
which is that they need
to be able to grow.

00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:10.470
And so if there are ways in
which that can be overcome

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:14.730
by providing more skilled
staff and supportive financing,

00:08:14.730 --> 00:08:18.180
then these kinds of
technologies that cut emissions

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.200
but also address other problems
that are energy access,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.240
air pollution, can go a long way

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:27.753
in helping develop a growing region.

